# Units masked on pro, one per line. Mask, not disable: a disabled unit can still be started by a
# socket, a timer or a dependency. Units that do not exist on the host are skipped.
#
# vip's pedal root is built from a package list, so most of this was never installed there and its
# list is short (devices/vip/rootfs/etc/pedal/masked-units). pro is a hand-installed Ubuntu Server,
# so everything Ubuntu enables by default is here and has to be turned off by name. piv's list
# (devices/piv/rootfs/etc/pedal/masked-units) is the closer precedent, and this is that list plus
# what pro actually runs, read from the host on 2026-10-31.
#
# The test applied to each one: does it wake up on its own, scan, index, write disk, phone home, or
# change the clock or the CPU's frequency behind the engine's back? A gig is not the time for any of
# those. What a reader should take from the length of this list is that an appliance built from a
# general-purpose server image carries a lot it does not need.
#
# One of these has a measurement behind it rather than a principle: fwupd, below. The rest are the
# principle, and the honest position is that none of them has been shown to cost this pedal anything
# — they are off because a thing that cannot wake up cannot interrupt the audio, which is cheaper
# than proving each one harmless.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- package maintenance
# Updates happen when you choose, not mid-gig.
apt-daily.service
apt-daily.timer
apt-daily-upgrade.service
apt-daily-upgrade.timer
unattended-upgrades.service
dpkg-db-backup.service
dpkg-db-backup.timer
man-db.timer
update-notifier-download.timer
update-notifier-motd.timer
motd-news.service
motd-news.timer
ubuntu-advantage.service
ua-reboot-cmds.service
ua-timer.timer
ua-timer.service

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- snaps
# Three snaps are installed (core24, snapd, hwctl) and none of them is the pedal's. snapd refreshes
# on its own schedule, which means downloading and remounting squashfs images under a playing
# engine. The .mount units are left alone: they are loop mounts that cost nothing once nothing
# refreshes them, and masking them would strand the files.
snapd.service
snapd.socket
snapd.seeded.service
snapd.apparmor.service
snapd.autoimport.service
snapd.core-fixup.service
snapd.recovery-chooser-trigger.service
snapd.system-shutdown.service
snapd.snap-repair.service
snapd.snap-repair.timer

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- first-boot provisioning
# `cloud-init status` already says "disabled", but the units are still enabled: a re-enable or an
# upgrade would bring them back, and what they do is rewrite the network configuration.
cloud-init-local.service
cloud-init-network.service
cloud-init-main.service
cloud-config.service
cloud-final.service
cloud-init-hotplugd.socket
cloud-init-hotplugd.service

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- the firmware update daemon
# MEASURED, not assumed. On 2026-11-01 the hour-long soak recorded **726 system-management
# interrupts in a single 10-second window** and zero in the other 355 samples — and pro's journal
# puts `fwupd-refresh.service` starting at 02:28:12, inside that window, running `fwupdmgr refresh`
# and activating `fwupd.service`. fwupd enumerates firmware: the UEFI capsule interface, the TPM,
# SPI flash, NVMe, USB. Reading those traps into firmware, and an SMI steals the core in a way the
# kernel cannot see, schedule around or account for — which is exactly the signature of the stall
# that failed pro's first hour on 2026-10-31 (2.0 ms of *CPU* time for one block, wall equal to CPU).
#
# So this is not hygiene, it is the leading candidate for the one unexplained failure on this host.
# It was in this file's prose from the start and missing from the list, which is why the timer still
# fired; `bench-results/2026-11-01-soak-pro-rt/` has the measurement.
#
# All three: the timer is what fires, but fwupd.service is dbus-activated
# (org.freedesktop.fwupd), so masking the timer alone leaves anything that asks over dbus able to
# start it. fwupd-refresh.service is `static` and pulled in by the timer.
fwupd.service
fwupd-refresh.service
fwupd-refresh.timer

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- the frequency and thermal daemons
# thermald is the dangerous one: it adjusts frequency and power limits to hold a temperature target,
# which is exactly the DVFS jitter pedal-tune's performance governor exists to remove, and it does
# it without telling anyone. Ubuntu's `linux-realtime` meta package *recommends* it, which is why
# rtpi installs the kernel with --no-install-recommends.
#
# irqbalance is not installed. It is named anyway because if it ever is, it will quietly undo
# pedal-tune's interrupt placement, and a masked unit that does not exist costs one skipped line.
thermald.service
irqbalance.service
power-profiles-daemon.service
tuned.service
upower.service

