# ---------------------------------------------------------------- deliberately NOT masked
#   chrony                  pro's only time source. Masking the clock on a machine that timestamps
#                           evidence is a self-inflicted wound (namchain host-slim's guard makes the
#                           same point). It steps the clock, which a soak's timestamps notice, so
#                           stop it by hand for a measurement that cares.
#   systemd-networkd, netplan-wpa-wlp0s20f3, wpa_supplicant, systemd-resolved
#                           WiFi is the way in: eno1 has no cable.
#   systemd-networkd-wait-online
#                           it only delays network-online.target, and a boot that waits is better
#                           than pro-boot validating a trial before the network is up.
#   ssh.service, ssh.socket the way in, both of them.
#   ufw                     a firewall is a security decision, not a latency one, and it is a
#                           boot-time oneshot that costs nothing while running. Ask before removing
#                           it; namchaind's TCP port (47810) already works through it.
#   apparmor, secureboot-db also security, also oneshots.
#   lvm2-monitor, dm-event.socket, blk-availability, finalrd
#                           the root volume is LVM. These are what notice a volume in trouble.
#   logrotate.timer         bounded daily work that keeps /var/log from growing without end. With
#                           rsyslog masked there is little left to rotate, but an unbounded log is
#                           worse than one daily wake-up on the housekeeping core.
#   systemd-pstore          reads the firmware's crash record once at boot. It is evidence.
#   polkit                  dbus-activated, and what is left of its callers is masked.
#   snap.hwctl.hwctl-daemon.hwctl-socket.socket
#                           HP's certification agent, from the `hwctl` snap. It CANNOT be masked:
#                           a mask is a symlink at /etc/systemd/system/<unit>, and this unit's own
#                           file is already at that path (snapd puts it there), so systemctl
#                           refuses rather than overwrite it. It is a socket, so nothing runs until
#                           something connects, and nothing does. The real answer is to remove the
#                           snap — `snap remove hwctl` — which is a decision about what is installed
#                           on the machine, not something this tree should do behind the owner's
#                           back. Until then it listens and does nothing.
